<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>The Go checks its GPS fix and uses ground speed alone to determine flight state.</h1>
  <p class="lede">To broadcast your position over ADS-L, skyBlip Go needs its own GPS position and the time. It checks the GPS receiver&rsquo;s output and waits for it to settle before transmitting. It uses ground speed to determine whether your aircraft is airborne or on the ground. That flight state determines the transmission rate and which updates are allowed.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The GPS fix</h2>

  <h3 id="fix">The Go checks the data before accepting a GPS fix.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The GPS receiver sends a solution twice a second, whether or not it can see the sky. The Go accepts that solution as a fix only after its own checks. Both sentences carrying the position and date must be present and fresh. The date must not be the 1980 placeholder some receivers send before they know it. The position must be within plausible reach of the previous one. The receiver must also report its solution as valid, and the Go adds its own checks.</p>
  <p class="lede">A Go uses a Quectel L76K receiver. A Bravo uses an Airoha AG3335. Both send their data in NMEA-format messages, called NMEA sentences. The Go and Bravo use the same parser to read them.</p>

  <h3 id="waiting">Three messages guide you while you wait.</h3>
  <p class="lede"><span class="mono">NO FIX</span> alone cannot tell you whether the antenna is under a hangar roof, the receiver is sending no data or a fix is twenty seconds away. The Go examines the sentences already received. It shows a message that tells you whether to move the unit, wait or have it checked:</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Message</th><th>What the Go receives</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>GPS OFF</th><td>no recent sentence from the receiver</td><td>have the unit checked. A clearer sky will not make data arrive from the receiver</td></tr>
        <tr><th>MOVE OUT</th><td>solutions arrive without a usable date</td><td>move the unit so the antenna has a clear view of the sky</td></tr>
        <tr><th>FINDS FIX</th><td>a usable date has been decoded</td><td>leave the unit where it is. The receiver has read a satellite&rsquo;s navigation message, but the geometry of the satellites being received is not yet sufficient to calculate a GPS fix</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede"><span class="mono">FINDS FIX</span> tells you that waiting can help. The receiver has decoded the date from the satellites&rsquo; navigation message, so it has progressed beyond receiving no usable signal. The status page also shows the time spent at the current stage, up to 9:59. The radar ring and the nearby list show only the message. This avoids redrawing part of the screen every second while the unit has no GPS fix.</p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "no-fix.png", alt: "The radar page with no position: the empty ring shows NO FIX and, below it, the receiver's progress towards a position fix" %>
    <figcaption>No fix yet, but the receiver has decoded a date</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 id="settle">The Go waits for the GPS fix to settle before transmitting.</h3>
  <p class="lede">A receiver starting cold can produce fixes whose position, altitude and especially ground speed wander for several seconds. Ground speed also triggers the airborne state. Transmitting too soon could broadcast a track the aircraft never flew, at the airborne rate while it is parked.</p>
  <p class="lede">The Go checks whether the fixes are settling rather than imposing a fixed wait. Each second, it predicts the next position from the previous fix. It assumes that speed and turn rate stay constant, then compares its prediction with the new fix. It starts transmitting after three predictions in a row miss by less than 12&nbsp;m. Twelve metres over one second corresponds to 12&nbsp;m/s, the takeoff threshold, so the error limit is tied to the flight decision it protects. A 2D solution never counts towards this check because it provides no measured altitude. If the receiver does not settle, transmission starts after 20&nbsp;s from the first fix, or 5&nbsp;s after a brief loss of fix. The wait has a ceiling because withholding every transmission would be worse.</p>

  <h3 id="deadline">The fix must arrive before 450&nbsp;ms from the start of the second.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The Go cannot use a solution for the current second until the receiver has sent its closing sentence. The first possible transmission is 450&nbsp;ms after the start of that second. The receiver starts sending about 70&nbsp;ms into it. At its startup rate of 9600&nbsp;baud, the 320 bytes of a fix take 333&nbsp;ms to arrive, leaving only 47&nbsp;ms of margin. A bench unit using 22 satellites completed its fix at 780&nbsp;ms. Every transmission scheduled before then was refused.</p>
  <p class="lede">The Go therefore switches the receiver to 115200&nbsp;baud, the highest rate documented for its configuration command. The same fix then takes 28&nbsp;ms to arrive. The command has no acknowledgement, so the Go checks for a correctly received sentence at the new rate. If none arrives, it returns to the old rate.</p>
  <p class="lede">The satellites page shows a signal bar for each satellite. The sentences carrying those levels also delay the fix, so the Go requests them only while that page is open, with one set every five seconds. The counts on the page still update every second.</p>
  <p class="lede">The receiver solves twice a second, on the full second and on the half. ADS-L stamps each position to the quarter second, to within 10&nbsp;ms of the moment the receiver measured it, and sends no position measured more than 500&nbsp;ms earlier. A solution on the full second therefore serves transmissions only until 490&nbsp;ms, keeping 10&nbsp;ms for the burst itself, and the half-second solution serves the rest of the slot. The receiver ignores a request for four solutions a second. At five a second, only the full second falls on a quarter. Our <%= link_to_page "ads-l/questions", label: "open questions to EASA" %> ask whether the rule means this.</p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "satellites.png", alt: "The satellites page: one bar per satellite grouped by constellation, against a dotted line at 40 dB-Hz" %>
    <figcaption>Signal levels are requested only while this page is open</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 id="pps">PPS marks the start of the second.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The GPS receiver provides a pulse per second, or PPS. Each pulse marks the start of a UTC second and is the reference for each <%= link_to_page "timing", label: "radio window" %>. If the pulses stop, Go stops transmitting 1.2&nbsp;s after the last pulse, in the first second without one. The radio windows keep running from the last pulse for 60&nbsp;s, for reception only. If no pulse returns, every page shows <span class="mono">NO TX</span> above <span class="mono">PPS</span> 11.2&nbsp;s after the last pulse, 10&nbsp;s after transmission stopped (<%= link_to_page "radar", label: "When Go does not transmit", anchor: "no-tx" %>). If a single pulse is missing, transmission pauses for that second without showing NO TX above PPS. Transmission resumes when the pulses return, provided the other requirements hold.</p>
  <p class="lede">Whether the L76K keeps producing pulses without a fix is still unverified. Its documentation describes the pulse but does not say whether losing a fix stops it. The chip supports either behaviour, and the Go does not configure it. A bench test is needed to establish what happens during a loss of fix.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The flight state</h2>

  <h3 id="flight">Ground speed alone determines flight state.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The Go decides whether the aircraft is airborne from the ground speed reported with each fix. It uses no altitude, vertical rate or barometer for that decision. Separate thresholds prevent a small change in speed from switching it back and forth:</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>State</th><th>Condition</th><th>Reason for the threshold</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Takeoff</th><td>12&nbsp;m/s or more (23&nbsp;kt)</td><td>a tug hurrying back to the grid does 10&nbsp;m/s. A glider lifts off on aerotow at about 20&nbsp;m/s</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Landing</th><td>under 8&nbsp;m/s for 10&nbsp;s</td><td>a sustained taxi speed lets the Go recognise a landing before the aircraft stops</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Landing</th><td>under 1&nbsp;m/s</td><td>a stop. The threshold is about three times the receiver&rsquo;s usual stationary speed noise</td></tr>
        <tr><th>TAXI on the screen</th><td>1.5&nbsp;m/s or more, on the ground</td><td>about a brisk walk. The display returns to GROUND below 1&nbsp;m/s</td></tr>
        <tr><th>In between</th><td>keep the previous state</td><td>the gap prevents repeated changes near a threshold</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/flight_state" %>

  <p class="lede">The gap between 12 and 1&nbsp;m/s matters in wind. A circling glider in a 15&nbsp;m/s wind can go from 5&nbsp;m/s upwind to 40&nbsp;m/s downwind on each turn. A single threshold between those speeds would repeatedly declare landings and takeoffs. Requiring 10&nbsp;s under 8&nbsp;m/s avoids that during the example turn: in a 20&nbsp;m/s wind, a glider circling at 22&nbsp;m/s spends about 3&nbsp;s of a 25&nbsp;s turn below the landing threshold.</p>
  <p class="lede">An earlier rule also declared flight above 2000&nbsp;m. That left a unit parked at Courchevel, at 2008&nbsp;m, permanently airborne. Ground speed avoids that altitude trap, but the quality of the fix still matters. Evidence for flight is reduced when the fix is poor, and a speed jump of more than fourfold between consecutive fixes is ignored. Evidence for the ground is never reduced, because reducing a speed must not manufacture a landing. The Go selects a flight state as soon as it gets its first fix after switching on. If the speed is between the thresholds, it selects the ground state. This matches the common case of switching on while being towed to the grid.</p>

  <h3 id="changes">What the state changes.</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>On the ground</th><th>Airborne</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Positions on the air</th><td>every 9 then 11&nbsp;s</td><td>every second</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Traffic alarm</th><td>no alerts</td><td>traffic is assessed for advisories, see <%= link_to_page "traffic", label: "traffic", anchor: "ground" %></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Updates, settings from the phone, flight log offload</th><td>allowed</td><td>new updates and log offload are refused. Settings that need a press are also refused. Settings from a trusted browser remain allowed</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Flight log</th><td>the preceding minute of taxi held in memory</td><td>recorded every second, including that preceding minute</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Flight timer</th><td>holds its reading</td><td>counts airborne time</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">A false takeoff blocks updates on the ground and uses the battery at the airborne transmission rate. A missed takeoff leaves an aircraft broadcasting at the ground rate while flying. Once the Go has declared flight, losing the GPS fix does not allow updates. The Go must first confirm that the aircraft is back on the ground. Losing reception behind a ridge therefore cannot allow an update to start in flight. If the unit has not obtained a fix since switching on, its state remains unknown and updates are allowed. This permits indoor bench tests.</p>
  <p class="lede">The flight timer counts airborne time since switching on, not since the last takeoff. It pauses at a landing and resumes at the next takeoff. A touch and go therefore does not reset the accumulated time, and a false landing loses only the seconds spent in the ground state.</p>

  <h3 id="no-roll">Low ground speed can hide a flight.</h3>
  <p class="lede">A paraglider flying at about 10&nbsp;m/s through the air has only 5&nbsp;m/s of ground speed into a 5&nbsp;m/s breeze. It can remain below the takeoff threshold throughout the flight. Hang gliders working a ridge and balloons can present the same problem. For those aircraft types, the Go broadcasts ADS-L&rsquo;s undefined state instead of on the ground. In ADS-L, that state is transmitted at the full rate. These aircraft are therefore never broadcast as parked. The ground-speed detector does not switch to the airborne state as a result. The flight log also follows a separate rule: it opens when the aircraft moves and closes after two minutes standing still.</p>
  <p class="lede">Barometric climb was once used to detect these flights. It was removed because gusts, pressure changes and spikes when the barometer dropped out caused false takeoffs in the hangar queue. Ground speed avoids those false detections, but it can miss flight at low ground speed. The undefined broadcast state and the separate logging rule address those limits without treating barometric climb as proof of flight.</p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The Go checks each fix on top of the receiver&rsquo;s validity flag</li>
    <li>GPS OFF, MOVE OUT and FINDS FIX tell you whether to investigate, move or wait</li>
    <li>Transmission starts after three predictions whose error against the received fix stays below 12&nbsp;m, with a ceiling of 20&nbsp;s after the first fix</li>
    <li>115200&nbsp;baud brings the fix in before the first possible transmission</li>
    <li>The Go declares takeoff at 12&nbsp;m/s and landing under 1&nbsp;m/s or after 10&nbsp;s under 8&nbsp;m/s</li>
    <li>Losing a fix in flight never counts as a landing</li>
  </ul>
</section>

